---
// Lesson 10.2a — Card Jam Kickoff: Lists & Arrays
// rpg jam kickoff: the card game jam begins; Lists in the starter.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>10.2a — Card Jam Kickoff: Lists &amp; Arrays</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Second jam, new data structure: the LIST. Your coins were one
    number, your inventory was a few bools — a deck of cards is
    MANY things, in order, changing. The starter ships a card game
    skeleton (GameManager.cs) with a secret: two of its methods are
    EMPTY, waiting for you.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Concept Warmup</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Open the starter's GameManager.cs</strong> (find it
    in the project — the card game scene). Two facts to verify with
    your own eyes:</p>
    <ol>
        <li>It is a singleton — <code>public static GameManager gm;</code>
            set in Awake — the same pattern your StatManager has
            used since <a href="2.1.html">2.1</a>. The game
            manager is THE manager; everything finds it.</li>
        <li>It holds about SIX Lists. Count them: the deck, the
            hand(s), maybe a discard pile, a play field. Every one
            is a <code>List&lt;Card&gt;</code> or similar. THE WHOLE
            GAME IS LISTS.</li>
    </ol>

    <p><strong>List vocabulary (the exam's, exactly):</strong></p>
    <table>
        <tr><th>Exam pseudocode</th><th>C# on a List</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
        <tr><td>aList[i]</td><td>hand[i]</td><td>Access the item at index i (starts at 1 in AP, 0 in C#!)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>APPEND(aList, item)</td><td>hand.Add(card)</td><td>Add at the end</td></tr>
        <tr><td>REMOVE(aList, i)</td><td>hand.RemoveAt(i)</td><td>Remove item at index i</td></tr>
        <tr><td>INSERT(aList, i, item)</td><td>hand.Insert(i, card)</td><td>Squeeze into position i</td></tr>
        <tr><td>LENGTH(aList)</td><td>hand.Count</td><td>How many items</td></tr>
    </table>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic</h3>
        <p>
            Your deck has 30 cards. You draw the top one: deck[0],
            REMOVE it, APPEND it to the hand. Why must the removal
            happen — what breaks at draw #31 if you never remove?
            (This is also why your coins could never be a List of
            coin objects — count what you keep, list what you
            ORDER.)
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Pitch</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>The jam task, read carefully:</strong> finish the
    starter's card game as YOUR version — your rules, your theme,
    your art swap — and ship it in five classes
    (<a href="10.2e.html">10.2e</a> build, <a href="10.2f.html">10.2f</a>
    showcase). The skeleton handles setup; the EMPTY methods
    (Shuffle() and AI_Turn() — verify they are empty in your copy!)
    are your unit's final boss.</p>

    <p><strong>Pitch card (10 min to write, 30s to pitch):</strong></p>
    <ol>
        <li>Theme sentence: "a card game about ___"
            (battles? cooking? dungeon crawling? dueling
            teachers?)</li>
        <li>The core loop: draw → play → resolve → repeat. Which
            part does the AI oppose?</li>
        <li>One twist that makes YOUR game not-just-war: costs?
            combos? a shared deck? one "cheat" card?</li>
    </ol>

    <p><strong>Vote + team (same rules as
    <a href="10.1a.html">10.1a</a>):</strong> teams 1–3, everyone
    writes code, everyone's game has the deck-draw loop playable by
    <a href="10.2e.html">10.2e</a>.</p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: List-thinking traps</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>"My hand shows card 3" — in C#, hand[3] is which
                visible card from the left? (Count from 0 — the
                exam counts from 1. Write BOTH answers down;
                you WILL need both.)</li>
            <li>REMOVE while traversing: you remove from a list
                WHILE looping over it. Why does every item AFTER
                the removal get visited wrong — and what is the
                standard fix (loop BACKWARD)?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Explain the starter's six Lists and what each holds</li>
            <li>Translate AP pseudocode list ops to C# on sight</li>
            <li>Explain why draw = REMOVE from deck + APPEND to hand</li>
            <li>Pitch a card game with a loop, a twist, and an AI</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-10/lists-arrays.html">Lists &amp; Arrays</a> — the full reference</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-10/scriptable-objects.html">ScriptableObjects</a> — tomorrow's tool</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="10.2b.html">10.2b — ScriptableObjects: Card Data as Assets</a></p>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="10.2a" title="10.2a Card Jam Kickoff: Lists &amp; Arrays — AP CS Principles" root=".." css="../style.css">
  <Fragment set:html={body} />
</Lesson>
